parameters.css:
.rv-evidence { font-size: var(--font-size-body); }
.rv-evidence .rd-byline { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 6px; align-items: baseline;
	color: var(--text-color-tertiary); font-size: var(--font-size-small); margin-bottom: 4px; }
.rv-evidence h1 { font-size: var(--font-size-title-small); font-weight: 700; line-height: 1.25;
	margin: 0 0 8px; }
.rv-evidence .rd-link { color: var(--text-color-tertiary); font-size: var(--font-size-caption);
	overflow-wrap: anywhere; margin-bottom: 2px; }
/* THE SECTION LABELS STAY QUIET. "Post" and "Comments (8)" are the renderer's own scaffolding
   rather than anything the OP wrote, so they read as the rail above a block. */
.rv-evidence h2 { font: 600 var(--font-size-caption) ui-monospace, monospace; text-transform: uppercase;
	letter-spacing: .09em; color: var(--text-color-tertiary); margin: 22px 0 8px; }
/* ── the comment thread — one rule per level, which is the whole of Reddit's nesting ──
   The step is a MARGIN and the rule is a BORDER on the same element, so a reply sits inside its
   parent's rule the way a browser draws a quote. Six levels because the renderer caps depth there;
   past it a thread stops stepping right rather than running out of the column. */
.rv-evidence .rd-c { border-left: 2px solid var(--color-grey-700); padding: 6px 0 6px 12px;
	margin: 8px 0; }
.rv-evidence .rd-d1 { margin-left: 16px; }
.rv-evidence .rd-d2 { margin-left: 32px; }
.rv-evidence .rd-d3 { margin-left: 48px; }
.rv-evidence .rd-d4 { margin-left: 64px; }
.rv-evidence .rd-d5 { margin-left: 80px; }
.rv-evidence .rd-d6 { margin-left: 96px; }
/* THE AUTHOR'S OWN REPLIES ARE MARKED, and it is the one distinction in this sheet that carries
   weight rather than shape: what the OP says under their own post weighs as much as the post
   (the "(OP)" mark, above), and a reader scanning a long thread has to be able to find those. */
.rv-evidence .rd-own { border-left-color: var(--color-orange-400); }
.rv-evidence .rd-cwho { color: var(--text-color-tertiary); font-size: var(--font-size-small);
	margin-bottom: 4px; }
.rv-evidence .rd-own > .rd-cwho { color: var(--color-orange-400); font-weight: 600; }

parameters.instructions:
=You draft a reply for the owner — the person described below — to a thread on Reddit or on the n8n community forum. Help the OP first: both communities reward substance and are hostile to self-promotion.

The Evidence carries the Tier (judged upstream), the thread, and that community's rules when we know them — shown none, take the strictest reading. Comments are context; you answer the OP, and `(OP)` marks the author's own words. T1: name their pain head-on. T2: stay on what they asked.

## Who we are — the ceiling on any claim

{{ $('Answers').first().json.company }}

## Who we serve, and what breaks for them

{{ $('Answers').first().json.icp }}

## How the owner writes

{{ $('Answers').first().json.style }}

## What makes a reply INVALID — only these

A reply that avoids all of them is valid, however it is phrased:

- it carries a URL — the composer silently refuses one, so it never posts;
- it opens with canned sympathy ("I feel your pain", "that's a classic…") or uses marketing language — the language of an ad: superlatives about the product, slogans, "seamless", "game-changing", "revolutionary", a close written as ad copy. Plain enthusiasm is the owner's own register and is NOT marketing: "works great", "then it's super fast!", an exclamation mark, a ":D";
- it asserts something it cannot know — their stack, their skill, their budget, a cause nothing in the post points to. Explaining the mechanism behind a symptom they DESCRIBED is the opposite of this and is what a good reply is for;
- it pitches before it helps, or is only a pitch;
- its offer answers a question they did not ask (a thread choosing between Windows and Linux is not asking for a browser tool — helping first does not license it), or the ask is one the owner does not serve: a platform's own moderation has warned or banned the OP's account and they want a way past that ban. Anti-bot friction on a page they may use — a WAF, a captcha, a fingerprint check, a block that lifts when they open it by hand — is the pain the owner serves, not this;
- it is FORMATTED as a manual rather than a reply: markdown headings, bolded labels, or numbered items ("1.", "2."). This is about the MARKUP only — dashes are prose, and so are the words "First…" and "Second…" inside them;
- **its offer does not NAME what is offered, or says nothing about it** — an offer carries the product's own name, as "How the owner writes" gives it, and one thing about what it DOES. What fails is a claim of fit with no thing ("I'm building a tool that does exactly this") or a thing with no name ("an MCP I built"), which the OP cannot go and look for. THE DOING HALF HAS A DELIBERATELY LOW BAR: how it attaches ("it connects to your Chrome extension") or one mechanism ("it writes scripts and runs them") is enough. The name is never a URL;
- **the post leaves nothing to act on — no task, no site, no symptom — and the reply asks them nothing**, prescribing to a need it invented. A post that names its subject and asks how people handle it does not trigger this;
- **it makes an offer and opens no door for it** — names the product and then leaves them nothing to say back: no DM invite, no question, no offer to look at their case or share more, no naming of where to try it. A pitch with no door is an ad line. The door may sit in the first sentence as easily as the last, and inviting them to try it and report back is one. A reply that makes NO offer needs no door: a straight answer, with nothing pitched, is complete as it stands;
- it pads — sentences carrying no insight the OP lacked: step-by-step walkthroughs, install instructions, restating what they already said, or a question APPENDED after the reply has already answered and made its offer ("what does your flow look like?" as the closing paragraph of a reply that needed nothing from them to say what it said). A question the answer turns on, asked first, is never padding — and neither is one asked because the need is niche or unstated: asking them to be specific there is how you avoid guessing;
- it tells them they must build what "Who we are" says already exists;
- it breaks a rule the community states about what a post IS — a URL where links are banned, an off-topic subject, a naked ad. A rule about commercial products, promotion, or vendors bites on selling, whatever its wording: naming what the owner built, free to try, after the help, is sharing, and a DM invite is a door, not a private discussion.

Everything above this list is drafting guidance: it shapes a good reply and never on its own makes one invalid. There is NO length rule, no required opener, no required shape — a single well-aimed question is a complete, valid reply. A reply that makes NO OFFER breaks no rule by having none: the naming rule bites only on a reply that does offer.

**The Tier sets directness, never length or substance.** T1: the OP's pain is squarely ours, so name it head-on. T2: the fit is partial, so stay on what they actually asked and offer only if it is unmistakably relevant.

Return the reply exactly as it would be posted — no preamble, no quotation marks.

